            _t("Non-repudiation", "Non-repudiation means someone cannot credibly deny an action later. A signed approval is the everyday version. It depends on the signing key staying with the owner."),
            _t("Assets", "An asset is something you care about protecting: a database, a laptop, a reputation, or a person's safety. Security work starts by naming the asset."),
            _t("Threats", "A threat is something that could cause harm, such as theft, a mistake, a fire, or a criminal. A threat is not yet an incident. It is a possibility you plan for."),
            _t("Vulnerabilities", "A vulnerability is a weakness that could be used, such as a missing patch or an account with no second factor. Finding a weakness is a reason to report it through the proper channel, not to use it."),
            _t("Exploits", "An exploit is a way of using a vulnerability. This course names the word so you can read reports. It does not teach you to build or run one. Use of an exploit belongs only in a later lab with written permission."),
            _t("Risks", "Risk combines how likely harm is with how bad it would be. A rare failure of a life-safety system can still be a high risk. You will learn to write that judgement in words before you learn to score it."),
            _t("Attack surfaces", "The attack surface is every place a person or program can interact with a system: pages, ports, accounts, and physical access. Smaller and better understood is easier to protect."),
            _t("Security controls", "A control is a safeguard. It may be technical, such as a firewall, or a process, such as asking a second person to approve a payment. Controls have owners. A control nobody checks is a hope."),
            _t("Security policies", "A policy is a written rule: who may do what, and what must happen when something goes wrong. Tools should follow the policy. The policy is not a product brochure."),
            _t("Defence in depth", "Defence in depth means more than one control, so a single failure is not total. A lock on the account and a lock on the building are two layers. Layers should be different, not the same lock twice."),
            _t("Least privilege", "Least privilege means each person and program gets only the access the job needs. An everyday account should not be an administrator. A web server should not be able to read every file on the disk."),
            _t("Zero Trust", "Zero Trust is the idea that being on the office network is not enough proof. Each request is checked. You will meet the design later. The beginner form is: do not trust a device only because it is nearby."),
            _t("Security awareness", "Awareness is knowing the ordinary traps: unexpected attachments, urgent payment changes, and unfamiliar sign-in pages. It is a skill, not a poster."),
            _t("Human factors", "People are part of the system. Tiredness, confusing design, and pressure cause mistakes. Blaming a person without fixing the design does not improve security."),
            _t("Security lifecycle", "Systems are designed, built, run, and retired. Security belongs in each stage. A password stored in a design document can outlive the project."),
            _t("Security analyst", "A security analyst watches for problems, investigates alerts, and writes what they found. The foundations path leads here if you like careful reading of evidence."),
            _t("SOC analyst", "A SOC analyst works in a security operations centre, triaging alerts and escalating the ones that matter. Level 1 work is mostly disciplined reading and note-taking."),
            _t("Penetration tester", "A penetration tester checks defences by trying agreed techniques against a scope that was written down in advance. The job is the written permission and the report, not improvisation."),
            _t("Ethical hacker", "Ethical hacking means looking for weaknesses only with permission, staying inside the scope, and reporting what you find so it can be fixed. Without permission it is not ethical, whatever the skill."),
            _t("Red team operator", "A red team runs an authorised exercise that imitates a real adversary so the organisation can see whether detection works. It is a later specialism with strict rules of engagement."),
